---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { LINKS } from '../config'

// The ladder. Order matters: each rung assumes the one above it.
// Add pages here as they get written. `ready: false` renders as
// "coming soon" and does not link anywhere.
const ladder = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'What Hermes is',
    blurb: 'The agent that reads your folders and does the work in them.',
    href: '#what',
    ready: true,
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'Getting started',
    blurb: 'Installing it and running it the first time.',
    href: null,
    ready: false,
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'Your first folder',
    blurb: 'Pointing Hermes at something real and watching it read.',
    href: '/start',
    ready: true,
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Channels and automations',
    blurb: 'Discord, Telegram, email, and work that runs on a schedule.',
    href: null,
    ready: false,
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'Profiles',
    blurb:
      'Keeping separate setups apart so 1 agent does not bleed into another.',
    href: LINKS.profiles,
    ready: true,
  },
]
---

<Base
  title="Hermes Agent"
  description="Hermes is the agent that walks an ICM folder structure. What it is, in plain words, and the order to learn it in."
  path="/hermes"
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <span class="badge">Hermes Agent</span>
      <h1>The agent that walks the folders</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        ICM gives you the structure. Hermes is what moves through it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what">What it actually is</h2>
      <p>
        Hermes is an agent that runs on your machine. You point it at a folder,
        it reads what is in there, and it does the work that folder describes.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the part worth sitting with. You are not pasting context into a
        chat window and hoping it sticks. The context lives in the folder, the
        agent reads the folder, and the next time you ask for something it is
        still there.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also connects out to Discord, Telegram and email, and it can run work
        on a schedule without you sitting there watching it.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>You do not need Hermes to start.</strong> The folder structure
          is the part that matters and you can build it tonight with nothing
          installed. <a href="/start">Do that first</a>. Hermes is what you add
          when you want the folders to actually do something on their own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The order to learn it in</h2>
      <p>
        Most guides drop you in the middle. This is the ladder, and each rung
        assumes the one above it.
      </p>

      <div style="display:grid;gap:0.75rem;margin:2rem 0">
        {
          ladder.map((step) => (
            <div
              class="card"
              style={`display:flex;gap:1.25rem;align-items:flex-start;${step.ready ? '' : 'opacity:0.55'}`}
            >
              <span
                style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--accent);font-size:0.95rem;padding-top:0.15rem"
              >
                {step.n}
              </span>
              <div style="flex:1">
                <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 0.25rem">
                  {step.ready && step.href ? (
                    <a href={step.href}>{step.title}</a>
                  ) : (
                    <span style="color:var(--muted)">{step.title}</span>
                  )}
                </h3>
                <p style="color:var(--muted);margin:0;font-size:0.95rem">
                  {step.blurb}
                </p>
              </div>
              {!step.ready && (
                <span
                  class="badge"
                  style="margin:0;font-size:0.6rem;white-space:nowrap"
                >
                  Being written
                </span>
              )}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <h2>A word about step 05</h2>
      <p>
        The profiles guide is thorough and it is worth reading, but it is
        further down this list for a reason. It assumes Hermes is installed and
        that you already know what a profile is. If you landed here from a video
        about second brains, start at the top instead.
      </p>

      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn" href="/start">Build 3 folders first</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={LINKS.profiles}>
          I have Hermes, take me to profiles
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
